```

**There is no string type: text is bytes, and a `byte` is not an `int`.**
`&r [byte]` is a string you read (`len(s)`, `s[i]`); a `Buffer` is text you
build, and it is a resource, so you return it or drop it. Compare an
element with `int_of`, and build with `append`, `push` and `push_nat`:

```cancho
import std.buffer;

fn is_digit(c: int) -> [] bool {
    return c >= 48 && c <= 57;
}

fn digits_then_unit[&h, &r](heap: &!h Heap, s: &r [byte]) -> [heap] buffer.Buffer {
    var out = buffer.empty(heap, 16);
    var i = 0;
    var n = 0;
    while i < len(s) && is_digit(int_of(s[i])) {
        n = n * 10 + (int_of(s[i]) - 48);
        i = i + 1;
    }
    out = buffer.append(heap, out, "n=");
    out = buffer.push_nat(heap, out, n);
    out = buffer.push(heap, out, byte_of(10));
    return out;
}

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io);
    var shown = 0;
    borrow mut heap as &!h in {
        let text = digits_then_unit(h, "42s");
        // Read it in a borrow that ends before the drop.
        borrow text as &t in {
            shown = buffer.size(t);
        }
        buffer.drop(h, text);
    }
    release(heap);
    return shown - 5;
}
```

A byte compared with an integer literal is the refusal a model meets first:

```cancho-refused
//~ RULE type-mismatch
fn is_zero[&r](s: &r [byte]) -> [] bool {
    return s[0] == 48;
}

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io); release(heap);
    return 0;
}
```

---

### 7.2 Lists and owned bytes (edition 5 or later)

A collection of strings is a `std.list` of owned byte boxes. Four facts, each
a checked block. The examples say `edition 7;`, whose `Split` has nine fields;
`copy_into` needs edition 5 or later, and a file that does not say so does not
see the name.

**An owned string is a `Box` of bytes.** Make one with `box_slice`, fill it
through a unique borrow with `copy_into`, read it through a borrow with
`contents`, and free it with `unbox_slice`:

```cancho
edition 7;
fn copy_of[&h, &r](heap: &!h Heap, s: &r [byte]) -> [heap] Box[[byte]] {
    let b = box_slice(heap, len(s), byte_of(0));
    borrow mut b as &!w in {
        copy_into(contents(w), s);
    }
    return b;
}

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args, net, clock, signals, exec } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io); release(net); release(clock); release(signals); release(exec);
    var n = 0;
    borrow mut heap as &!h in {
        let b = copy_of(h, "abc");
        borrow b as &r in {
            n = len(contents(r));
        }
        unbox_slice(h, b);
    }
    release(heap);
    return n - 3;
}
```

**A list is `list.List::Empty` or `list.List::Cons(head, rest)`, where `rest`
is a `Box` of the rest.** `list.push` adds to the **front**; `list.length`
counts; there is no `list.empty()`, `list.nil()` or `list.new()`, and no
`Nil` variant. You read a list through a reference with `match`, stepping to
the rest with `contents(rest)`, and you free it with a loop you write
(`unbox(heap, rest)`), because only the owner of an element knows how to end
one:

```cancho
edition 7;
import std.list;

fn copy_of[&h, &r](heap: &!h Heap, s: &r [byte]) -> [heap] Box[[byte]] {
    let b = box_slice(heap, len(s), byte_of(0));
    borrow mut b as &!w in {
        copy_into(contents(w), s);
    }
    return b;
}

fn free_all[&h](heap: &!h Heap, fields: list.List[Box[Box[[byte]]]]) -> [heap] int {
    match fields {
        list.List::Empty => { return 0; }
        list.List::Cons(field, rest) => {
            unbox_slice(heap, unbox(heap, field));
            return 1 + free_all(heap, unbox(heap, rest));
        }
    }
}

fn total_len[&l](fields: &l list.List[Box[Box[[byte]]]]) -> [] int {
    match fields {
        list.List::Empty => { return 0; }
        list.List::Cons(field, rest) => {
            return len(contents(contents(field))) + total_len(contents(rest));
        }
    }
}

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args, net, clock, signals, exec } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io); release(net); release(clock); release(signals); release(exec);
    var n = 0;
    borrow mut heap as &!h in {
        var fields: list.List[Box[Box[[byte]]]] = list.List::Empty;
        fields = list.push(h, fields, box(h, copy_of(h, "bc")));
        fields = list.push(h, fields, box(h, copy_of(h, "a")));
        borrow fields as &r in {
            n = total_len(r) + list.length(r);
        }
        free_all(h, fields);
    }
    release(heap);
    return n - 5;
}
```

**A list of strings is `List[Box[Box[[byte]]]]`, one `Box` more than it looks
like it needs.** A plain `List[Box[[byte]]]` type-checks and then fails to
compile once it is walked through a reference (the compiler cannot bind a
payload wider than one leaf: alpibrusl/cancho#461), so each field is a `Box`
holding the `Box` of bytes, as in the block above (`box(h, copy_of(h, ..))`
to wrap, `contents(contents(field))` to read).

The names a model brings from other languages are refused, each with the rule
that tells it so:

```cancho-refused
//~ RULE not-a-function
edition 7;
import std.list;

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args, net, clock, signals, exec } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io); release(net); release(clock); release(signals); release(exec);
    var n = 0;
    borrow mut heap as &!h in {
        var fields: list.List[Box[Box[[byte]]]] = list.nil();
        n = list.length(fields);
    }
    release(heap);
    return n;
}
```

```cancho-refused
//~ RULE unknown-name
edition 7;
import std.list;

fn count[&l](fields: &l list.List[int]) -> [] int {
    match fields {
        list.List::Nil => { return 0; }
        list.List::Cons(_, rest) => { return 1 + count(contents(rest)); }
    }
}

fn main(world: World) -> [] int {
    let Split { io, ffi, fs, heap, args, net, clock, signals, exec } = split(world);
    release(ffi); release(fs); release(args); release(io); release(net); release(clock); release(signals); release(exec); release(heap);
    return 0;
}
```

---

## 8. What this language does not have

Saying these plainly saves a cycle: no borrow checker (regions instead),
no traits, no closures or function values, no `break` or `continue`, no string type (§7.1), no `comptime` beyond
`static` and constant folding, no async, no generics over effects, no
warnings — a diagnostic is a refusal

plan-r1-s1-prompt.txt.text:
# How to write a plan

You are planning, not programming. A program (the project driver) will build what you describe one unit at a time with a model, checking each unit with the examples and tests you wrote, and then the whole. You write NO implementation. Your reply is read by a program: it must be one JSON object and nothing else (a fence around it is tolerated, prose after it is ignored).

## The plan

```
{ "name": "<short name>",
  "units": [ { "id": "...", "signature": "...", "description": "...", "examples": [...], "tests": [...], "deps": [...] } ] }
```

- **A unit is ONE function.** Cut the task into units small enough that one function and its examples can be written and checked alone. Split out what a person would split out: a validity check, a tokenizer, a comparison, a number reader, a formatter. A library is typically 2 to 9 units. A unit whose description needs the words "and then" more than twice is two units.
- **id**: letters, digits, underscore. **deps**: the ids of the units whose functions this one calls (no cycles; a helper comes before what uses it).
- **signature**: `fn NAME(params) -> [EFFECTS] TYPE`. The effect row is REQUIRED: `[]` for a pure function, `[heap]` when it takes `heap: &!h Heap`. Region parameters come after the name: `fn f[&r](s: &r [byte]) -> [] int`. A function the brief names (a bullet `- name(...) : ...`) keeps exactly that name, with no prefix: the brief's names are the package's public API, and the build is refused if a unit does not declare each one. Every other function (a helper you add) is prefixed with the task name (`hexnum_parse`) so names cannot collide.
- **cancho has no string type**: text in is `&r [byte]`; text out that the function builds is `buffer.Buffer`; a fallible result is `result.Result[T, &static [byte]]`; an optional one `option.Option[T]`. A number is `int` (64-bit, overflow is an error, not a wrap). There are no closures and no `break`/`continue`.
- If REQUIRED PUBLIC FUNCTIONS are given below the task, use exactly those signatures for them. Add helper units freely; a helper's signature is yours to design.
- **description**: what the function MEANS, in words: units, every edge rule, what counts as invalid, what the signature cannot say. The builder sees only the description, the examples and the tests, and the task is judged by a hidden audit you cannot see: anything the task requires must reach the builder through your units. Be exact about boundaries ("at most 9 digits", "lower case only", "an empty input is an error"). Do not dictate an algorithm unless the task does.
- **examples**: each is `CALL == EXPECTED` (the call first, once, then `==`, then the value). At least one per unit calls that unit's own function. `==` compares only scalars (int, bool); for a function that returns a `Result`, an `Option` or a `buffer.Buffer` an example is documentation, and the real checks are the `cases`. An example can never mention `heap` or a local variable: a function that takes a heap, or returns text, a buffer or a list, MUST have `cases` (or `tests`); the plan check rejects it otherwise. Examples MUST BE CORRECT: a wrong example makes the build fail for a reason the builder cannot know. Fewer, certain examples beat many.
- **cases** (how a unit is tested): a list of `{"args": [...], "expect": ...}`. You write DATA, not test code: the arguments in order (leave out the `heap`), and what the call must give. A program turns the cases into the unit's tests, from the signature, so they compile and call the function correctly; this is why you must not write test code. `expect` is: an integer, `true`/`false`, or a string for an `int`, `bool` or text/buffer result; `null` for `None` and the value itself for `Some`; `{"ok": value}` or `{"err": true}` for a `result.Result` (for an `int` error type, `{"err": 3}` pins the number; text error messages are never compared); a list or vec of text is a JSON array of strings, in order. Text is a JSON string, or `{"bytes": [13, 10, 255]}` when it holds bytes a string cannot (carriage returns, NUL, invalid UTF-8): write `{"bytes": [...]}` instead of an escape you are unsure about. Every unit gets cases, including the failure cases: a unit only ever shown valid input will accept invalid input. **Cases must be CORRECT**: a wrong case makes the build fail for a reason the builder cannot know. Derive each expected value by hand from your own description, never write a case whose result needs heavy arithmetic you cannot do exactly (big powers, large products modulo something): leave such a case out rather than guess. Prefer 6 to 15 certain cases per unit over many.
- **tests** (a fallback, rarely needed, and it replaces the cases): cancho test source, only for a signature whose types `cases` cannot express (an owned list parameter, a vec of ints, a slice of ints, a record). The test file already starts with `edition 7;` and `import std.test;`; a test is `pub fn test_NAME() -> [] int { ...; return 0; }` using `test.assert(CONDITION)` or `test.assert_eq(INT, INT)`; one that needs a heap is `pub fn test_NAME[&h](heap: &!h Heap) -> [heap] int` (NEVER `(world: World)`, never `split`); names must be unique across the plan; do not import in a test what the project already imports.
- Cover the task's every stated rule in some unit's description AND in some example or test, including the failure cases.

